This package repackages dsh-free-search by DDDMUC, used under the MIT License.
The original project lives at:

    https://github.com/DDDMUC/dsh-free-search

Upstream publishes this plugin prebuilt, with no sources, so vendor/ holds its
published lib/ output verbatim and scripts/build.mjs adapts it. Every edit is
an asserted exact-string rewrite, so a changed upstream line fails the build
rather than silently shipping something unintended.

English defaults. Upstream is tuned for Chinese, and four values carry that:

  - `ACCEPT_LANG` is sent on every engine request through one shared fetch
    helper, so `zh-CN,zh;q=0.9,en;q=0.8` steers DuckDuckGo, Bing, SearXNG and
    AnySearch alike. This is the highest-leverage one; it now sends
    `en-US,en;q=0.9`.
  - `lang` (the settings UI language) defaults to `en` rather than `zh`.
  - `bingMarket` defaults to `en-US` rather than `zh-CN`, in the config schema
    and again in the hardcoded fallback that a cleared setting falls back to.
  - `region` (DuckDuckGo's `kl` parameter) now defaults to `us-en`; upstream
    leaves it unset.

The default engine changes with them, for the same reason. Upstream picks Bing
and says why: "most stable, optimized for Chinese (zh-CN)". That reason does
not survive the switch to English. Measured here on "what is the capital of
Portugal", DuckDuckGo returned en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lisbon first while Bing
returned Capital One and a UK radio station, behind bing.com/ck/a redirect
wrappers. DuckDuckGo rate-limits more often, which the automatic fallback
chain already handles by moving on to Bing and the rest.

Nothing else in the plugin emits Chinese. Every other Chinese string in the
host half is a comment, and the model-facing text — tool descriptions and the
injected system-prompt section — is already English. The browser half keeps
both dictionaries and switches on `lang`, so Chinese remains available; it is
simply no longer the default.

Identity. The browser bundle names itself three times: the `__ModuleLoader__`
loader id (the web boot manifest looks a bundle up by the installed package
name), a `settings.plugin.item` slot entry id (which must be unique within its
slot), and a `data-plugin` attribute on its injected stylesheet. All three are
retargeted to `@my-dsh/search`. The `/api/dsh-free-search-settings` bridge
prefix is deliberately left alone: host and client must agree on it, and it is
an internal path rather than an identity.

SearXNG is bring-your-own-instance. Upstream ships six public instances, and
none of them work — not because of transient rate limiting, but because
SearXNG disables the JSON output format by default and its bot limiter
rejects anonymous `format=json`. Probed 2026-08-20: all six upstream defaults
plus ten more public instances returned 429 to a SINGLE request, 403, HTML
instead of JSON, or nothing. Zero served JSON.

That cost more than one failing engine, because searxng sits in the automatic
fallback chain: every fallback spent six pointless requests on third parties,
up to 8s each when an instance hangs rather than refusing fast. The default
list is now empty, so it fails in milliseconds with "no instances configured"
— a case upstream already handles — and the system-prompt line tells the model
the engine needs a configured instance instead of advertising it as free and
keyless. The engine itself is untouched and works well against a self-hosted
SearXNG; point `searxngInstances` at your own.

Provider wiring. Upstream's cordis.patch.yml notes that `web.searchProvider`
must point at its provider id, but does not ship that override — so a fresh
install still routes web_search to DeepSeek and appears to do nothing. This
package's patch sets it.

Nothing is bundled: the host half imports only @deepseek-ai/dsh-settings,
@deepseek-ai/dsh-tools, and @deepseek-ai/schemastery, all of which the harness
profile already resolves.

The tools/ directory of the upstream repository (a local engine-switcher with
Windows .cmd launchers) is not carried; the settings page and the
/free-search-engine command cover the same ground.

